# SurrealQL conventions (SurrealDB 2.x)

Write SurrealQL that matches how SurrealDB 2.x actually behaves. These rules are
verified against the runtime in `runtime/surrealdb/`.

## Schema definition
- Prefer `DEFINE TABLE <t> SCHEMAFULL` for governed records; use `SCHEMALESS`
  only for genuinely open payloads.
- Define every field: `DEFINE FIELD <name> ON <table> TYPE <type>`.
- Use the real type system: `option<T>` for nullable, `array<T>`, `record<other>`
  for links, `object`, `datetime`, `number`, `bool`, `string`, `array<float>`.
- Use `FLEXIBLE TYPE object` only when a nested object is intentionally open.
- Make definitions idempotent with `IF NOT EXISTS` (e.g. in migrations).
- Enforce enums with `ASSERT $value IN [...]` (SurrealQL has no native enum).
- Defaults and computed values: `DEFAULT time::now()`, `VALUE time::now()`,
  `READONLY` for immutable fields.

## Record IDs and links
- IDs are `table:id`. Quote non-simple ids with backticks: `` _migration:`0001_x` ``.
- Reference other records with `record<table>` fields, not raw strings.

## Graph
- Create edges with `RELATE a->edge->b SET ...`.
- Define edge tables as `DEFINE TABLE <edge> TYPE RELATION FROM <a> TO <b>`.
- Traverse with arrows: `SELECT ->authorized_for->tool.name AS tools FROM agent:x`.

## Transactions and events
- Wrap multi-statement mutations in `BEGIN TRANSACTION; ... COMMIT TRANSACTION;`.
- Triggers: `DEFINE EVENT <e> ON TABLE <t> WHEN $event = "CREATE" THEN { ... };`
  use `$event`, `$before`, `$after`, `$value` inside the body.

## Access control (v2)
- Use `DEFINE ACCESS <name> ON DATABASE TYPE RECORD` with `SIGNUP`/`SIGNIN`.
  Do NOT use the removed `DEFINE SCOPE` / `DEFINE TOKEN` v1 syntax.
- Gate tables/fields with `PERMISSIONS FOR select|create|update|delete ...`.
- Hash secrets with `crypto::argon2::generate` / compare with `crypto::argon2::compare`.

## Don't
- Don't invent functions or operators; if unsure, check the SurrealQL reference.
- Don't store timestamps as strings — use `datetime` and `time::now()`.
- Don't redefine the same enum/type in two files in the same package/namespace.
